The ability of the meniscus to perform these functions depends on its sophisticated mechanical properties, which are directly tied to its internal structure and biochemical composition. The meniscus exhibits marked anisotropy; its mechanical response varies based on the direction of loading \citep{seitz2013stressrelaxationresponse}, largely due to the circumferential alignment of collagen fibers. It also demonstrates viscoelastic behavior, with time-dependent responses such as stress relaxation and creep under sustained loads \citep{chaudhuri2016hydrogelstunable}, allowing it to adapt to both static and dynamic forces. Regional variability is another critical feature: the outer (red) zone is more fibrous and stiffer due to dense type I collagen, whereas the inner (white) zone is softer and more cartilage-like, enriched with proteoglycans and type II collagen \citep{koch2022mechanicalproperties}. Furthermore, the tissue's stiffness increases with strain rate \citep{zhang2005electrospinninggelatin}, allowing the meniscus to resist sudden, high-impact forces. 